{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Why do you cry to Me.</b> It is difficult: to whom should one cry if not to HaShem his Elohim, especially in a time of distress, as it is written (Jonah 2:3) ״קָרָאתִי מִצָּרָה לִי״, (Psalms 118:5) ״מִן הַמֵּצַר קָרָאתִי יָהּ״ — and if the reason is that he prayed at length, surely as long as one has not been answered by the One who answers in time of trouble, one should not relent from prayer. Further, it appears to me that his prayer was in fact accepted, for HaShem said to him <b>Lift up your staff</b> etc.; if so, what is the point of this response, saying <b>Why do you cry to Me</b>? It is further difficult, his saying <b>Speak to the children of Israel and let them journey</b> — to where shall they journey, if a pursuer is behind them and the sea is before them? And if the intention is after the sea is split, then He should have said ״Lift up your staff״ etc. first and afterward say ״Speak to the children of Israel״ etc." "<b>Now</b>, the matter may be explained according to the statement of our Sages, may their memory be blessed (Exodus Rabbah 21:7), that Israel stood in judgment: ״these are idolaters and these are idolaters.״ And it is a well-known thing that the power of mercy is [drawn from] the good deeds that a person does below — they add power to the attribute of mercy, and conversely, because of our many iniquities, they diminish the power, as it says (Deuteronomy 32:18) ״צוּר יְלָדְךָ תֶּשִׁי״. Now, since El Elyon saw that Israel was being prosecuted against by the attribute of justice — and it is true that HaShem desired to vindicate Israel, but there was no power in the attribute of mercy on account of their deeds as mentioned — therefore He said to Moses an enduring reply: <b>Why do you cry to Me</b> — meaning, the matter does not depend on Me; though I desire to perform a miracle, since they are not worthy, the attribute of justice prevents it, and there is no power in mercy against the attribute of justice that is preventing it. And He said to him <b>Speak to the children of Israel</b> — meaning, this is the counseled advice to strengthen the side of kindness and mercy: speak to the children of Israel and let them strengthen themselves in faith with all their heart, and let them journey to the sea before it splits, relying on their trust that I will perform a miracle for them, and through this means the attribute of mercy will prevail. <b>And you, lift up your staff</b> — meaning, through the instrument of a good deed the miracle will be performed for them and the sea will be split, for great is this trust and faith to tip the balance for good. And you will find that so it was: the first righteous one was Nachshon son of Amminadav, who entered [the sea] up to his neck, and the sea did not split until he said ״כִּי בָאוּ מַיִם עַד נָפֶשׁ״, as our Sages, may their memory be blessed, stated (Sotah 37a). And through this the verses are reconciled properly. And it appears to me to say that HaShem designated for him to tell them the reason for the prevailing of justice over them — because they had diminished the faith in their hearts and said ״הֲלֹא טוֹב לָנוּ אֶת עֲבוֹד מִצְרַיִם״; therefore HaShem commanded to act against this iniquity with the reinforcement of faith in full force. Also in this He alluded to them that they should know what surrounds the prevailing of the attribute of justice anew."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:00.603850676Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}